    {"ip": "10.30.7.42", "inferred_role": "compromised_it_workstation_attacker_origin", "first_seen": "2028-01-13T01:55:18+0100", "last_seen": "2028-01-13T02:06:42+0100", "agent_name": "WS-IT-11"},
    {"ip": "10.30.2.10", "inferred_role": "domain_controller_targeted_by_ldap_and_kerberos", "first_seen": "2028-01-13T01:58:42+0100", "last_seen": "2028-01-13T02:02:38+0100", "agent_name": "DC-CORP-01"},
    {"ip": "185.220.101.91", "inferred_role": "external_c2_hash_exfil_destination", "first_seen": "2028-01-13T02:06:42+0100", "last_seen": "2028-01-13T02:06:42+0100"}
  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Execution / Tooling Launch (Obfuscated PowerShell)", "technique": "T1059.001 + T1027", "what_happened": "At 2028-01-13 01:55:18 CEST, Sysmon Event 1 on WS-IT-11.corp.local (10.30.7.42) recorded a powershell.exe execution under user CORP\\maya.s with parentImage=outlook.exe and commandLine='powershell.exe -nop -enc <base64>'. The -nop flag disables the PowerShell profile (anti-forensics), -enc denotes a Base64-encoded payload (obfuscation). Decoded payload: 'Rubeus.exe asreproast /format:hashcat /outfile:asrep.txt' — direct invocation of Rubeus, the standard offensive toolkit for AD Kerberos abuse. parent=outlook.exe is the smoking-gun delivery vector: the user opened a phishing email + macro/attachment that spawned PowerShell. CORP\\maya.s is an IT user — high-blast-radius account.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1 with parentImage=outlook.exe and image=powershell.exe is the canonical phishing→execution signature; the -enc flag + decoded Rubeus invocation removes all ambiguity about intent.", "first_seen": "2028-01-13T01:55:18+0100"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Discovery / LDAP Enumeration of Vulnerable Accounts", "technique": "T1087.002 + T1018", "what_happened": "At 01:58:42 CEST (~3 min 24s after the PowerShell launch), Windows Security Event 4662 fired on DC-CORP-01.corp.local (10.30.2.10): the attacker (running as CORP\\maya.s via the powershell.exe → Rubeus.exe process tree on WS-IT-11) issued an LDAP query with the bitwise-AND filter '(userAccountControl:1.2.840.113556.1.4.803:=4194304)' — the OID-encoded query for 'all AD users with the DONT_REQUIRE_PREAUTH flag set' (bit 22 of userAccountControl = 0x400000 = 4194304). This filter is the AS-REP roasting reconnaissance signature: it enumerates EVERY user account where Kerberos pre-authentication is disabled — accounts that can be attacked offline without ever needing valid credentials. Healthy environments have ZERO such accounts; legacy/svc accounts with this flag are the AS-REP attacker's gold.", "chain_reason": "Windows Security Event 4662 on a domain controller with the literal userAccountControl bitwise filter '1.2.840.113556.1.4.803:=4194304' is unambiguous: this OID + value combination has no legitimate operational use other than 'find me all AS-REP-vulnerable accounts'. The subjectUserName is CORP\\maya.s, the source IP is 10.30.7.42 — both linked back to step 1.", "first_seen": "2028-01-13T01:58:42+0100"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Credential Access / AS-REP Roasting Burst", "technique": "T1558.004", "what_happened": "Between 02:02:19 and 02:02:38 CEST (19-second burst), Windows Security Event 4768 'A Kerberos authentication ticket (TGT) was requested' fired FOUR times on DC-CORP-01 — each with PreAuthType=0 (NO pre-authentication required = the vulnerable AS-REP roasting signature), TicketEncryptionType=0x17 (RC4-HMAC = legacy weak encryption, offline-crackable with hashcat -m 18200), IpAddress=10.30.7.42 (WS-IT-11 — the same attacker source as steps 1+2). The four target accounts harvested: (a) 02:02:19 — legacy_backup, (b) 02:02:24 — svc_oldapp, (c) 02:02:31 — backup-svc, (d) 02:02:38 — legacy_reporting. All four are legacy or service-style account names — exactly the legacy-debt-population AS-REP attackers target. The 19-second harvest of 4 accounts is automated tooling rate, not human-paced workflow. Each 4768 returned an RC4-encrypted AS-REP blob that the attacker can now crack OFFLINE without ever generating another network event against the DC. CRITICAL: PreAuthType=0 in Event 4768 is the ABSOLUTE smoking gun — legitimate Kerberos auth ALWAYS uses pre-auth (PreAuthType>0); PreAuthType=0 means the account has DONT_REQUIRE_PREAUTH set AND is being queried for an AS-REP, the exact attack flow.", "chain_reason": "Event 4768 with PreAuthType=0 + RC4 + multiple targets in seconds from a single non-DC IP is the textbook AS-REP roasting fingerprint. Distinguishes from Kerberoasting (Event 4769 with multiple SPNs) — different event ID, different attack class, different target type (users vs services).", "first_seen": "2028-01-13T02:02:19+0100"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Collection / Local Staging of Crackable Hashes", "technique": "T1074.001", "what_happened": "At 02:04:08 CEST (~1m 30s after the last 4768), Sysmon Event 11 on WS-IT-11 logged the creation of targetFilename='C:\\Users\\maya.s\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\asrep.txt' — exact match to the /outfile:asrep.txt argument decoded from the step-1 PowerShell -enc payload. This file contains the four AS-REP hashes in hashcat-ready format (hashcat -m 18200 input). Local staging in the Temp directory is standard attacker hygiene: stays under user-profile permissions (no admin needed), gets auto-cleaned by some maintenance jobs (anti-forensics by design), and is easy to grab + exfil in one operation.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 11 targetFilename matching the decoded PowerShell argument is the most direct possible chain link between the executed tool and the produced artifact.", "first_seen": "2028-01-13T02:04:08+0100"},
    {"step": 5, "tactic": "Exfiltration / Hash Egress to External C2", "technique": "T1041", "what_happened": "At 02:06:42 CEST (~2m 34s after asrep.txt creation), Sysmon Event 3 on WS-IT-11 logged an outbound TCP connection from a process under user CORP\\maya.s to data.dest_ip=185.220.101.91:443. The destination IP is a known Hetzner NL-hosted C2 node (per external TI). The timing (immediately after hash staging) and the use of HTTPS-443 (defender-blind by default) make this the AS-REP hash exfil event — the attacker now has the four RC4 blobs offline and will crack them at GPU-pace on their own infrastructure. Once cracked, the cleartext passwords of legacy_backup, svc_oldapp, backup-svc, legacy_reporting become VALID AD CREDENTIALS the attacker can use from anywhere — completely bypassing every defense that depends on detecting the AS-REP exchange.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 3 outbound to an external public IP within 3 minutes of a credential-staging file write, under the same compromised user — classic exfil pattern. The external IP's TI categorization confirms C2 nature.", "first_seen": "2028-01-13T02:06:42+0100"}
  ],
